ok to place  the trump presidency  in the context of a century of  authoritarian history  and i had written many things predicting  he would follow a kind of authoritarian  playbook referring to 21st century  authoritarianism where you come to  office  via elections and you manipulate  elections to try and stay there  and so it was intended as a kind of  warning  that no country is immune from  the lure of leaders who appear on the  scene and legitimize  existing extremist uh and antidemic  and anti-democratic tendencies and make  uh racism part of their platforms and  legitima is also the idea that violence  is a way to  cause problems to deal with enemies of  the state  and to move history forward and we saw  this coming to a climax in january 6.  so um the book is divided into three  periods uh the fascist period the age of  military coups  and what i call new authoritarianism  which starts with the end of the cold  war  and i chose to focus mainly on  right-wing strong men so i'm just giving  you the context now  in part because of my training in  fascism and i was also interested in  individuals who  damage or destroy democracies rather  than  kind of inherited power as in china or  north korea  but clearly these right as in  personality cults and  tactics of violence the the right and  the left-wing  uh authoritarian things unfold in  dialogue and i make that clear in the  book  and the core of the book are chapters on  what i call the authoritarian playbook  the tools of rule  and propaganda corruption virility the  myth of national greatness and violence  and each one goes over a hundred years  and this was quite challenging to write  it at times i had wished i  i had just done a biographical approach  you know everything on mussolini  everything on hitler  but i wanted the reader to be able to  see for example with propaganda  what changes and what stays the same  over a hundred years  and just to define my terms i use the  term strong strongmen to refer  t